Structural Pattern:



1. Adapter Pattern 


Problem:
Two incompatible interfaces need to work together.


1. Our common interface

Our application expects every payment provider to implement PaymentProcessor.

interface PaymentProcessor {
    void pay(double amount);
}


Let's say your application supports multiple payment providers: PayPal and PhonePe.
The problem is that each third-party API has a different method name.



2. PayPal third-party API
We cannot modify this class because it's provided by PayPal.


class PayPal {

    public void makePayment(double amount) {
        System.out.println(
            "Paid $" + amount + " using PayPal"
        );
    }
}



3. PhonePe third-party API

Again, different method name.

class PhonePe {

    public void sendMoney(double amount) {
        System.out.println(
            "Paid ₹" + amount + " using PhonePe"
        );
    }
}


Now we have:

Our Application
       |
       | expects
       ↓
PaymentProcessor
       |
       | pay()
       |
---------------------------
|                         |
PayPal                   PhonePe
makePayment()            sendMoney()



Problem: Our application expects pay(), but:

PayPal has makePayment()
PhonePe has sendMoney()

They are incompatible.






4. PayPal Adapter

class PayPalAdapter implements PaymentProcessor {

    private PayPal paypal;

    public PayPalAdapter(PayPal paypal) {
        this.paypal = paypal;
    }

    @Override
    public void pay(double amount) {
        paypal.makePayment(amount);
    }
}



5. PhonePe Adapter

class PhonePeAdapter implements PaymentProcessor {

    private PhonePe phonePe;

    public PhonePeAdapter(PhonePe phonePe) {
        this.phonePe = phonePe;
    }

    @Override
    public void pay(double amount) {
        phonePe.sendMoney(amount);
    }
}



6. Our Payment Service

Now our application only knows about PaymentProcessor.

class PaymentService {

    public void processPayment(
            PaymentProcessor processor,
            double amount) {

        processor.pay(amount);
    }
}

It doesn't care whether it's PayPal or PhonePe.


public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        PaymentService service =
                new PaymentService();

        // PayPal
        PayPal paypal = new PayPal();

        PaymentProcessor paypalProcessor =
                new PayPalAdapter(paypal);

        service.processPayment(
                paypalProcessor,
                100
        );


        // PhonePe
        PhonePe phonePe = new PhonePe();

        PaymentProcessor phonePeProcessor =
                new PhonePeAdapter(phonePe);

        service.processPayment(
                phonePeProcessor,
                500
        );
    }
}



Output
Paid $100 using PayPal
Paid ₹500 using PhonePe
The important part to understand:
                PaymentService
                      |
                      | PaymentProcessor
                      ↓
              processor.pay()
                      |
             -----------------
             |               |
             ↓               ↓
      PayPalAdapter    PhonePeAdapter
             |               |
             ↓               ↓
       makePayment()    sendMoney()
             |               |
             ↓               ↓
          PayPal           PhonePe










2. Facade Pattern 

Idea: Provide a simple interface to hide a complex subsystem.
Let's use a Travel Booking example.

Without Facade, the client needs to call multiple services:

Client
  |
  |-- FlightService
  |-- HotelService
  |-- PaymentService



With Facade:

Client
  |
BookingFacade
  |
  |-- FlightService
  |-- HotelService
  |-- PaymentService



// Complex subsystem 1
class FlightService {

    public void bookFlight() {
        System.out.println("Flight booked");
    }
}


// Complex subsystem 2
class HotelService {

    public void bookHotel() {
        System.out.println("Hotel booked");
    }
}


// Complex subsystem 3
class PaymentService {

    public void makePayment() {
        System.out.println("Payment successful");
    }
}



Now create the Facade:

class BookingFacade {

    private FlightService flightService;
    private HotelService hotelService;
    private PaymentService paymentService;

    public BookingFacade() {
        flightService = new FlightService();
        hotelService = new HotelService();
        paymentService = new PaymentService();
    }


    public void bookTrip() {

        paymentService.makePayment();
        flightService.bookFlight();
        hotelService.bookHotel();

        System.out.println("Trip booked successfully");
    }
}



Client
Instead of doing this:


Client simply does:

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        BookingFacade facade = new BookingFacade();

        facade.bookTrip();
    }
}



Output
Payment successful
Flight booked
Hotel booked
Trip booked successfully


Interview one-liner:
Facade Pattern provides a simple unified interface to a complex subsystem, hiding the underlying complexity from the client.

Remember:
Facade = Simple interface → hides complex operations


Used in:

Microservices
API Gateway
Service layer





3. Decorator Pattern

Idea: Add new behavior to an object without modifying its original class.

Example: Coffee 

Start with:

Coffee

Add-ons:

Coffee
  ↓
Milk
  ↓
Sugar
  ↓
Chocolate

Instead of creating classes like:

MilkCoffee
SugarCoffee
MilkSugarCoffee
MilkSugarChocolateCoffee

We wrap the original object with decorators.



Decorator Pattern with the Coffee example, kept interview-friendly.

1. Base Interface
interface Coffee {
    String getDescription();
    double getCost();
}


2. Basic Coffee
class SimpleCoffee implements Coffee {

    public String getDescription() {
        return "Coffee";
    }

    public double getCost() {
        return 50;
    }
}


3. Decorator
abstract class CoffeeDecorator implements Coffee {

    protected Coffee coffee;

    CoffeeDecorator(Coffee coffee) {
        this.coffee = coffee;
    }
}
The decorator wraps another Coffee object.


4. Milk Decorator
class MilkDecorator extends CoffeeDecorator {

    MilkDecorator(Coffee coffee) {
        super(coffee);
    }

    public String getDescription() {
        return coffee.getDescription() + ", Milk";
    }

    public double getCost() {
        return coffee.getCost() + 10;
    }
}


5. Sugar Decorator
class SugarDecorator extends CoffeeDecorator {

    SugarDecorator(Coffee coffee) {
        super(coffee);
    }

    public String getDescription() {
        return coffee.getDescription() + ", Sugar";
    }

    public double getCost() {
        return coffee.getCost() + 5;
    }
}


6. Usage
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Coffee coffee = new SimpleCoffee();

        coffee = new MilkDecorator(coffee);

        coffee = new SugarDecorator(coffee);

        System.out.println(coffee.getDescription());
        System.out.println(coffee.getCost());
    }
}

Output:

Coffee, Milk, Sugar
65
